@meta
id: 21-06
title: End where the next one starts
chapter: 21
type: lesson
skills: macro-record, macro-count
par: 45
minutes: 7
vscode: a regex replace on pipes and spaces, then fix the ends by hand
open: refund-cases.csv

@brief
QA sent the refund test cases as a markdown table. The test loader
wants CSV. Every row, header included, loses its pipes:

    | ord_101 | cus_7 | 1200 |    →    ord_101,cus_7,1200

The `|---|---|---|` separator line is not a row. It goes.

The catch is in the shape of the edit. Cleaning the end of a line
leaves the cursor at the end of the line, and `j` keeps the column. If
the macro then starts with a motion that only looks forward, it starts
in the wrong place and quietly does the wrong thing. A macro has to
finish where the next replay needs to begin.

@keys
| `dw` | delete the pipe and the space after it |
| `f\|` then `h` | onto the space before the next pipe |
| `3s` | replace three characters and start inserting |
| `$xx` | delete the last two characters |
| `j0` | down a line, then to column one |

@hints
Delete the separator line first, go back to the top, and record on the
header row. The macro is `dw`, then two rounds of `f|h3s,` `Esc`, then
`$xx`. Now think about where the cursor is.
--
It is at the end of the line. If the recording ends with a bare `j`,
the next replay starts at the end of the next line and `dw` eats the
wrong thing. End with `j0` instead.
--
`jdd` `gg`, then `qadwf|h3s,<Esc>f|h3s,<Esc>$xxj0q`, then `12@a`.

@solution
    j dd gg   qa dw f|h 3s, <Esc> f|h 3s, <Esc> $xx j0 q   12@a   :wq<Enter>

Thirty-nine keystrokes.

Every macro has a contract with itself: it starts from a position, and
it must leave the cursor in that same kind of position for the next
run. There are two honest ways to keep the contract.

Normalise at the start: open with `0` or `^` (lesson 1 and 5) and the
macro does not care what came before. Or normalise at the end: finish
with `j0`, or `j^`, or a search that lands on the next target. Either
is fine; forgetting both is the commonest way a macro fails on line two
after working on line one. If a replay does something strange, the
first thing to check is where the previous one left the cursor.

You may have noticed that `12@a` ran the last `j0` onto nothing and
stopped. That is the safety property from the previous lesson doing its
job at the end of the file.

